edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445955 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445955 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445955 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445955 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445955 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "85c16440-4f36-5799-80af-9e7a27fdee13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445956 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c85f83e-b227-560c-a6a8-ac2b41f8a28a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445956 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=130.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=5,a=90,$ and ${y}_{0}=130.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-90),\\,y(0)=60,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=60<a=90.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445957 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445957 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445957 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a70316a-6e6e-5988-9abb-850863435cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445957 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=210.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=30,a=200,$ and ${y}_{0}=210.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-30(y-200),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=100<a=200.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445958 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate at which a cup of chocolate cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the chocolate in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "502009e8-d4ec-5acf-8f7e-4d70ee8a14f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445958 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={16}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-16).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445959 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y>16$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "daeefc4f-6d08-5a08-a1e7-5aed6435b59f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445959 #edb/ref 1074 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=960-60y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-60(y-16).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=60,$ and $a=16.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>16$ for all $t>0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
